      "source": [
        "### Choosing the number of clusters\n",
        "\n",
        "Silhouette is highest at k = 2 for both matrices, but that split is degenerate: it peels off a handful of\n",
        "extreme shares and leaves everything else in one giant cluster, which is not a useful segmentation. The\n",
        "dendrograms displays the risk-return tree separates into about **four** groups and the RFM\n",
        "tree into about **three**. I choose k value by *interpretability plus structure*, not silhouette alone,\n",
        "and I kept the choice in one place so it is easy to change and justify."
      ],
      "id": "iJEZGbZl9A5g"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "# Chosen cluster counts. Change these if your size/profile tables argue for something else.\n",
        "# (k=2 wins on silhouette but is a one-big-cluster-plus-outliers split, so I use the dendrogram structure.)\n",
        "K_RISK = 4\n",
        "K_RFM  = 3\n",
        "print(\"Chosen cluster counts -> risk-return:\", K_RISK, \"| RFM:\", K_RFM)\n",
        "\n",
        "ward_risk = pd.Series(AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=K_RISK, linkage=\"ward\").fit_predict(X_risk),\n",
        "                      index=common, name=\"Ward_Risk\")\n",
        "ward_rfm  = pd.Series(AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=K_RFM, linkage=\"ward\").fit_predict(X_rfm),\n",
        "                      index=common, name=\"Ward_RFM\")\n",
        "print(\"Ward risk-return sizes:\", ward_risk.value_counts().sort_index().tolist())\n",
        "print(\"Ward RFM sizes:\", ward_rfm.value_counts().sort_index().tolist())"
